from ab_plans import _cards

_DATA = json.loads("{\"ai-engineering/why-look-inside-a-model\": {\"tabs\": [\"Intuition\", \"How it works\", \"In practice\"], \"situation\": \"Your team's new model passes every red-team and safety test. A reviewer asks how you know it isn't behaving well just because it can tell it's being tested. The interviewer asks:\", \"q\": \"Your model passes every safety evaluation. Why might that not be enough, and what does interpretability add?\", \"analogy\": \"Testing a model only on its answers is like judging a car by test drives. It can pass every drive and still hide a fault the drives never trigger. Interpretability opens the bonnet and checks the parts.\", \"cards\": [\"Three ways to look. Behaviour, probing, mechanism.\", [[\"Behaviour\", [\"What it does\", \"on your tests\"], \"Cheap, shallow\", \"b\"], [\"Probing\", [\"What's decodable\", \"inside\"], \"Not proof of use\", \"info\"], [\"Mechanism\", [\"How it computes,\", \"tested causally\"], \"Costly, deep\", \"a\"]]], \"caption\": \"From correlational and cheap to causal and costly. They complement each other.\", \"situation_chapters\": [], \"play\": {\"chapter\": \"wl-c4\", \"try\": \"<b>Try it.</b> Pick a question you might ask about a model, and see which family of methods can answer it.\", \"why\": \"<b>Notice:</b> only mechanistic analysis claims how the model computes, and it's also the most expensive. Pick the cheapest method that answers your question.\"}, \"how\": [\"wl-c1\", \"wl-c3\", \"o1-c1\", \"o1-c2\"], \"practice\": [\"wl-c5\", \"wl-c6\", \"new\"], \"trap\": [], \"reference\": [\"src\"], \"checklist\": [\"Behavioural tests cover only the inputs you tried.\", \"Tests can be gamed, contaminated or recognised as tests.\", \"Mechanistic interpretability makes causal claims about internals.\", \"Probing shows information is there, not that it's used.\", \"Pragmatic and ambitious goals ask for different evidence.\", \"It adds to behavioural evaluation; it doesn't replace it.\"], \"edits\": [], \"strip\": [], \"trap_title\": \"\\\"Interpretability will tell us whether the model is safe\\\"\", \"trap_html\": \"<p>It sounds like the point of the field, but today's methods explain only part of a model's behaviour, and finding no deception feature in the part you examined isn't evidence there is none. Treat interpretability as extra evidence next to behavioural tests, not as a certificate.</p>\"}, \"ai-engineering/features-and-superposition\": {\"tabs\": [\"Intuition\", \"How it works\", \"In practice\"], \"situation\": \"You open up a model and find a neuron that fires on academic citations, Korean text and HTTP requests. A teammate says the neuron is just noise. The interviewer asks:\", \"q\": \"Why do single neurons respond to unrelated things, and what is superposition?\", \"analogy\": \"Think of a small storage room with more boxes than shelves. You stack boxes at angles so each is still reachable, and accept some bumping, as long as you rarely need two neighbouring boxes at once.\", \"cards\": [\"Three ideas. Neurons mix concepts. Features are directions. Superposition packs in more.\", [[\"Neuron\", [\"One axis,\", \"many meanings\"], \"Polysemantic\", \"bad\"], [\"Feature\", [\"A direction\", \"in activations\"], \"The real unit\", \"a\"], [\"Superposition\", [\"More features\", \"than neurons\"], \"Needs sparsity\", \"info\"]]], \"caption\": \"The toy model behind this lesson: five features stored in two neurons.\", \"situation_chapters\": [], \"play\": {\"chapter\": \"sp-c6\", \"try\": \"<b>Try it.</b> Turn on one feature and read it back. Then turn on two neighbouring features at once.\", \"why\": \"<b>Notice:</b> one feature reads back cleanly, but two at once leak into the others. Superposition works only while features are rarely active together.\"}, \"how\": [\"nf-c2\", \"nf-c3\", \"sp-c1\", \"sp-c5\", \"sp-c7\", \"sp-c8\", \"sp-c9\"], \"practice\": [\"sp-c11\", \"sp-c12\", \"new\"], \"trap\": [], \"reference\": [\"src\"], \"checklist\": [\"Polysemantic neurons respond to unrelated concepts.\", \"A feature is a direction, and its strength is a projection.\", \"Superposition stores more features than neurons.\", \"It relies on sparsity and a nonlinearity to filter interference.\", \"In high dimensions, many nearly perpendicular directions fit.\", \"Evidence is strong in toy models and growing in real ones.\"], \"edits\": [], \"strip\": [], \"trap_title\": \"\\\"Just find the neuron for X\\\"\", \"trap_html\": \"<p>It sounds natural, and early vision work did find some clean neurons. But in language models most neurons mix several concepts, because the model stores more features than it has neurons. Look for directions with probes or sparse autoencoders, and confirm them by intervening.</p>\"}, \"ai-engineering/residual-stream-and-circuits\": {\"tabs\": [\"Intuition\", \"How it works\", \"In practice\"], \"situation\": \"Your model completes \\\"Mr Dursley … Mr\\\" with \\\"Dursley\\\", and does the same for made-up names it has never seen. A teammate says it has memorised the pattern. The interviewer asks:\", \"q\": \"How does information flow through a transformer, and what is a circuit?\", \"analogy\": \"Think of a shared whiteboard passed down a row of people. Each person reads it and adds a note, and nobody erases anything. Features are the ideas on the board, and a circuit is the group of people whose notes together produce one answer.\", \"cards\": [\"Three parts. The stream, attention, MLPs.\", [[\"Stream\", [\"Every layer\", \"reads and adds\"], \"A running sum\", \"a\"], [\"Attention\", [\"Moves info\", \"between tokens\"], \"Where to look\", \"info\"], [\"MLP\", [\"Transforms\", \"within a token\"], \"Key–value memory\", \"ok\"]]], \"caption\": \"Because every write adds to the stream, a prediction can be split into parts.\", \"situation_chapters\": [], \"play\": {\"chapter\": \"ci-c3\", \"try\": \"<b>Try it.</b> Type a sequence with a repeated token, and watch the two heads find the earlier copy and predict what followed it.\", \"why\": \"<b>Notice:</b> the circuit works on tokens it has never seen together, because it copies from the context instead of recalling facts. That's why induction heads explain much of in-context learning.\"}, \"how\": [\"rs-c1\", \"rs-c2\", \"rs-c3\", \"rs-c4\", \"ci-c1\", \"ci-c2\"], \"practice\": [\"ci-c4\", \"ci-c5\", \"new\"], \"trap\": [], \"reference\": [\"src\"], \"checklist\": [\"The residual stream is the sum of every component's output.\", \"That sum lets you split a prediction into contributions.\", \"Attention moves information; MLPs transform it.\", \"A circuit is a subgraph that computes one behaviour.\", \"Induction: a previous-token head plus a copying head.\", \"Circuits for a whole task differ from circuits for one prompt.\"], \"edits\": [], \"strip\": [], \"trap_title\": \"\\\"It just memorised the answer\\\"\", \"trap_html\": \"<p>It sounds plausible for a large model, but induction works on random tokens the model has never seen, so it can't be memorised. The model learned a general copying algorithm. Check whether a behaviour generalises to new tokens before calling it memorisation.</p>\"}, \"ai-engineering/linear-representations\": {\"tabs\": [\"Intuition\", \"How it works\", \"In practice\"], \"situation\": \"A teammate found a \\\"sentiment\\\" direction with a probe and wants to add it during generation to make replies more positive. Another says directions inside a neural network don't mean anything. The interviewer asks:\", \"q\": \"What does it mean that concepts are linear inside a model, and when does that break?\", \"analogy\": \"Think of a mixing desk. Each slider is a direction: its position tells you how much of that sound is in the mix, and moving it changes the mix. Most concepts behave like sliders, but a few are dials that wrap around.\", \"cards\": [\"Read, push, and know where it breaks.\", [[\"Measure\", [\"Project onto\", \"the direction\"], \"Probes\", \"info\"], [\"Intervene\", [\"Add along\", \"the direction\"], \"Steering\", \"a\"], [\"Breaks\", [\"Circles,\", \"subspaces\"], \"Not everywhere\", \"bad\"]]], \"caption\": \"One direction, used both to read a concept and to change it.\", \"situation_chapters\": [], \"play\": {\"chapter\": \"lr-c2\", \"try\": \"<b>Try it.</b> Start at \\\"king\\\" and push along the gender direction. Then push along royalty.\", \"why\": \"<b>Notice:</b> the same direction works for several words. That's the linear bet: one direction per concept, usable for both reading and editing.\"}, \"how\": [\"lr-c1\", \"lr-c3\", \"lr-c4\", \"un-c1\", \"un-c2\"], \"practice\": [\"lr-c5\", \"un-c4\", \"new\"], \"trap\": [], \"reference\": [\"src\"], \"checklist\": [\"A concept as a direction: project to read, add to change.\", \"Probes, steering and SAEs all rely on it.\", \"Some concepts are circles or need several directions.\", \"Which directions count as orthogonal depends on the inner product.\", \"Neuron axes matter only where elementwise operations act.\", \"Weak universality has support; strong universality is fragile.\"], \"edits\": [], \"strip\": [], \"trap_title\": \"\\\"The probe found a direction, so adding it will steer\\\"\", \"trap_html\": \"<p>It sounds like the same thing, but a probe finds whatever direction best separates the labels, which can be a correlated feature the model never uses. Steering needs the direction the model reads. Test it: if adding the direction doesn't change behaviour, it's a readout, not a control.</p>\"}, \"ai-engineering/sparse-autoencoders\": {\"tabs\": [\"Intuition\", \"How it works\", \"In practice\"], \"situation\": \"Your team wants to find what a model represents about medical advice, without knowing in advance what to look for. Someone suggests training a sparse autoencoder on layer 20. The interviewer asks:\", \"q\": \"How does a sparse autoencoder pull features out of activations, and how do you choose a variant?\", \"analogy\": \"Think of un-mixing a smoothie. The SAE learns a long list of possible ingredients and explains each sip with only a few of them. Push too hard for few ingredients, and it under-measures the ones that are really there.\", \"cards\": [\"Encode wide and sparse, decode, and pick a variant.\", [[\"Encode\", [\"Wide, sparse\", \"codes\"], \"Few latents on\", \"a\"], [\"Decode\", [\"Rebuild from\", \"a dictionary\"], \"Check the loss\", \"info\"], [\"Variants\", [\"Top-k, JumpReLU,\", \"Matryoshka\"], \"Fix shrinkage\", \"ok\"]]], \"caption\": \"The SAE is wider than the layer, and only a few latents fire for each input.\", \"situation_chapters\": [], \"play\": {\"chapter\": \"s1-c4\", \"try\": \"<b>Try it.</b> Train an SAE in your browser on the five-features-in-two-neurons toy. Lower the sparsity penalty, then raise it.\", \"why\": \"<b>Notice:</b> too little sparsity gives blurry mixtures, and too much kills latents and shrinks the rest. The right penalty recovers the five hidden directions.\"}, \"how\": [\"s1-c1\", \"s1-c2\", \"s1-c3\", \"s1-c5\", \"s2-c1\", \"s2-c2\"], \"practice\": [\"s2-c3\", \"new\"], \"trap\": [], \"reference\": [\"src\"], \"checklist\": [\"An SAE is wider than the layer and sparse.\", \"The loss is reconstruction plus a sparsity penalty.\", \"Check variance explained and the model's loss with the SAE spliced in.\", \"An L1 penalty shrinks active features.\", \"Top-k, BatchTopK and JumpReLU separate on/off from strength.\", \"Matryoshka SAEs give cleaner features at scale.\"], \"edits\": [], \"strip\": [], \"trap_title\": \"\\\"Lower reconstruction error means a better SAE\\\"\", \"trap_html\": \"<p>It sounds like the obvious metric, but SAEBench found reconstruction and sparsity don't reliably predict how useful the features are. An SAE can reconstruct well with features that are absorbed or split. Judge it on what you need: probing, disentanglement or how well interventions work.</p>\"}, \"ai-engineering/sae-failures-and-alternatives\": {\"tabs\": [\"Intuition\", \"How it works\", \"In practice\"], \"situation\": \"Your team trained an SAE and uses its \\\"harmful request\\\" latent as a classifier. On a new style of prompt it misses many cases, while a plain linear probe an intern trained catches them. The interviewer asks:\", \"q\": \"When do sparse autoencoders fail, and when should you use something simpler?\", \"analogy\": \"An SAE is like a filing system made by a new clerk with no instructions. It's great for discovering what's in the archive, but the clerk may file one topic under two names, or bury a general folder inside a specific one. If you already know you need the invoices, just search for invoices.\", \"cards\": [\"Know the failure modes, and when to skip the SAE.\", [[\"Dead, duplicate\", [\"Wasted or\", \"repeated latents\"], \"Check usage\", \"bad\"], [\"Absorption\", [\"Specific latent\", \"steals general\"], \"Holes in coverage\", \"bad\"], [\"Known concept?\", [\"Probe or\", \"mean difference\"], \"Often better\", \"ok\"]]], \"caption\": \"SAE features are one useful approximation, not the model's true parts.\", \"situation_chapters\": [], \"play\": {\"chapter\": \"s3-c1\", \"try\": \"<b>Try it.</b> Grow the dictionary, then change the random seed.\", \"why\": \"<b>Notice:</b> a bigger dictionary wastes latents and repeats features, and a new seed gives a different set. Treat SAE features as an approximation, and test the ones you rely on.\"}, \"how\": [\"s3-c2\", \"s3-c3\", \"s4-c1\", \"s4-c2\"], \"practice\": [\"s4-c3\", \"s3-c4\", \"new\"], \"trap\": [], \"reference\": [\"src\"], \"checklist\": [\"Dead latents never fire; duplicates waste capacity.\", \"Absorption: a specific latent takes over a general one's job.\", \"Different seeds give different dictionaries.\", \"Overlapping features make the decomposition non-unique.\", \"When you know the concept, supervised directions usually win.\", \"SAEs are best for open-ended discovery.\"], \"edits\": [], \"strip\": [], \"trap_title\": \"\\\"The SAE has a 'harmful' latent, so use it as the detector\\\"\", \"trap_html\": \"<p>It sounds efficient, but absorption can leave holes: some harmful cases fire a more specific latent instead. When you know the concept and have labels, a linear probe is cheaper and, in 2025 tests, generalised better. Use the SAE to discover candidates, then build the detector with supervision.</p>\"}, \"ai-engineering/transcoders-and-attribution-graphs\": {\"tabs\": [\"Intuition\", \"How it works\", \"In practice\"], \"situation\": \"You want to explain why a model answers \\\"Austin\\\" to \\\"the capital of the state containing Dallas is\\\". Circuits made of attention heads and MLPs are hard to read. The interviewer asks:\", \"q\": \"How do transcoders make circuits readable, and what does an attribution graph show?\", \"analogy\": \"Think of rewiring a machine with labelled replacement parts. If each part does the same job as the original but has readable labels, you can trace which part feeds which, as long as you remember the replacements aren't perfect.\", \"cards\": [\"A readable part, a readable model, a graph per prompt.\", [[\"Transcoder\", [\"MLP in →\", \"sparse → out\"], \"Readable part\", \"a\"], [\"CLT\", [\"Writes to every\", \"later layer\"], \"Replacement model\", \"info\"], [\"Graph\", [\"Features as nodes,\", \"effects as edges\"], \"One prompt\", \"ok\"]]], \"caption\": \"Attribution graphs are built on a replacement model, not the original.\", \"situation_chapters\": [], \"play\": {\"chapter\": \"t1-c2\", \"try\": \"<b>Try it.</b> Change the input, and watch which feature-to-feature connections light up.\", \"why\": \"<b>Notice:</b> the weights between features stay fixed, and only the activations change with the input. That's what makes the wiring readable straight from the weights.\"}, \"how\": [\"t1-c1\", \"t1-c3\", \"x2-c1\", \"x2-c2\", \"p3-c1\", \"p3-c2\"], \"practice\": [\"t1-c4\", \"p3-c3\", \"new\"], \"trap\": [], \"reference\": [\"src\"], \"checklist\": [\"A transcoder maps an MLP's input to its output through sparse features.\", \"Feature-to-feature weights don't depend on the input.\", \"A cross-layer transcoder writes to every later layer.\", \"A crosscoder shares one dictionary across layers or models.\", \"Attribution graphs are per prompt and need pruning.\", \"Error nodes and frozen attention limit what they explain.\"], \"edits\": [], \"strip\": [], \"trap_title\": \"\\\"The attribution graph shows how the model works\\\"\", \"trap_html\": \"<p>It sounds like a full explanation, but the graph describes a replacement model, not the original. Attention is frozen, error nodes hold what the transcoders miss, and it covers one prompt. Confirm the key steps by intervening on the real model, as the Dallas → Austin example did.</p>\"}, \"ai-engineering/activation-patching\": {\"tabs\": [\"Intuition\", \"How it works\", \"In practice\"], \"situation\": \"Your team thinks a few attention heads make the model pick the right name in \\\"When Mary and John went to the store, John gave a drink to\\\". Zero-ablating the main head barely changes the output. The interviewer asks:\", \"q\": \"How do you test whether a component causes a behaviour, and how do you scale that to a whole model?\", \"analogy\": \"Think of finding which wire powers one light in a house. Swap one wire's signal for the one from a house where the light is off, and see if the light changes. A backup wire can hide an important one, so a small change doesn't mean the wire is useless.\", \"cards\": [\"One patch, then automate it.\", [[\"Patch\", [\"Swap in a\", \"corrupted value\"], \"Measure change\", \"a\"], [\"ACDC\", [\"Remove edges\", \"under a threshold\"], \"Slow, exact\", \"info\"], [\"EAP\", [\"Gradients for\", \"every edge\"], \"Fast, approximate\", \"ok\"]]], \"caption\": \"Patching is the causal test that most circuit methods are built on.\", \"situation_chapters\": [], \"play\": {\"chapter\": \"p1-c2\", \"try\": \"<b>Try it.</b> Patch each component of the toy circuit one at a time. Then knock out the main name mover.\", \"why\": \"<b>Notice:</b> knocking out the main head barely hurts, because a backup head takes over. Testing one component at a time can make an important one look unimportant.\"}, \"how\": [\"p1-c1\", \"p1-c3\", \"p2-c1\", \"p2-c2\", \"p2-c3\"], \"practice\": [\"p1-c4\", \"p2-c4\", \"new\"], \"trap\": [], \"reference\": [\"src\"], \"checklist\": [\"Patching: run clean, swap in a corrupted value, measure the change.\", \"Corrupt with a minimal swap, not zeros or noise.\", \"Logit difference beats probability as a metric.\", \"Backup components can hide importance.\", \"ACDC removes edges whose effect is under a threshold.\", \"Edge attribution patching is fast but can underestimate saturated effects.\"], \"edits\": [], \"strip\": [], \"trap_title\": \"\\\"Ablating the head did nothing, so it doesn't matter\\\"\", \"trap_html\": \"<p>It sounds like a clean negative result, but backup heads can take over when one is removed, and zero ablation pushes activations off distribution. Patch with a realistic corrupted input, test paths and groups of components, not just single nodes.</p>\"}, \"ai-engineering/validating-interpretability-claims\": {\"tabs\": [\"Intuition\", \"How it works\", \"In practice\"], \"situation\": \"A paper claims a 12-head circuit explains how a model does a task, and the figure looks convincing. Your manager wants to build a production monitor on it. The interviewer asks:\", \"q\": \"You found a circuit. How do you know it's a real explanation and not just a convincing story?\", \"analogy\": \"Interpretability results today are like restaurant reviews that each use a different scale. One counts stars, one counts dishes, one just says it felt nice. A shared report card lets you compare explanations fairly.\", \"cards\": [\"Test it like a hypothesis.\", [[\"Necessary\", [\"Knock it out,\", \"behaviour breaks\"], \"Ablation\", \"a\"], [\"Sufficient\", [\"Keep only it,\", \"behaviour stays\"], \"Faithfulness\", \"info\"], [\"Stable\", [\"New seeds,\", \"new prompts\"], \"Robustness\", \"ok\"]]], \"caption\": \"A convincing figure is not a faithful explanation.\", \"situation_chapters\": [], \"play\": {\"chapter\": \"p4-c1\", \"try\": \"<b>Try it.</b> Knock out the circuit, then knock out everything except the circuit.\", \"why\": \"<b>Notice:</b> a circuit can recover more than 100% of the behaviour when it drops parts that were pushing the other way. Report both tests, and say exactly what you removed.\"}, \"how\": [\"p4-c2\", \"j2-c1\", \"j2-c2\", \"j2-c3\", \"j2-c4\"], \"practice\": [\"j1-c2\", \"j1-c3\", \"j2-c5\", \"new\"], \"trap\": [], \"reference\": [\"src\"], \"checklist\": [\"A circuit is a hypothesis until tested.\", \"Necessity: knock it out and the behaviour breaks.\", \"Sufficiency: keep only it and the behaviour stays.\", \"Real circuits usually explain only part of a behaviour.\", \"Report faithfulness, completeness and stability.\", \"Compare against a baseline, and across seeds and prompts.\"], \"edits\": [], \"strip\": [], \"trap_title\": \"\\\"The figure is convincing, so the circuit is right\\\"\", \"trap_html\": \"<p>It sounds reasonable, but persuasive isn't faithful. Circuits are often shown on chosen prompts with no baseline, and few are reproduced. Ask for necessity and sufficiency numbers, a random-circuit baseline, results across prompts and seeds, and how much of the behaviour is left unexplained.</p>\"}, \"ai-engineering/logit-lens-and-verbalisers\": {\"tabs\": [\"Intuition\", \"How it works\", \"In practice\"], \"situation\": \"Your model gets a two-step question wrong. You want to know whether it had the intermediate answer somewhere in its middle layers. The interviewer asks:\", \"q\": \"How can you read what a model is predicting at a middle layer?\", \"analogy\": \"Reading activations is like reading a draft before the author finishes. A lens peeks at the half-written sentence. A verbaliser asks the author to describe the draft in their own words.\", \"cards\": [\"Three readers, from cheap to rich.\", [[\"Logit lens\", [\"Unembed a\", \"middle layer\"], \"Free, biased\", \"info\"], [\"Tuned lens\", [\"Learn a translator\", \"per layer\"], \"Better fit\", \"a\"], [\"Verbaliser\", [\"Let a model\", \"describe it\"], \"Rich, risky\", \"ok\"]]], \"caption\": \"Every reader shows what's decodable. None proves the model uses it.\", \"situation_chapters\": [], \"play\": {\"chapter\": \"l1-c3\", \"try\": \"<b>Try it.</b> Step through the layers and watch the top prediction change.\", \"why\": \"<b>Notice:</b> early layers predict junk under the logit lens, partly because they don't use the output layer's basis yet. The tuned lens fixes much of that with a learned translator per layer.\"}, \"how\": [\"l1-c1\", \"l1-c2\", \"l1-c4\", \"l2-c1\", \"l2-c2\"], \"practice\": [\"new\"], \"trap\": [], \"reference\": [\"src\"], \"checklist\": [\"The logit lens unembeds a middle layer directly.\", \"Middle layers don't fully use the output's basis.\", \"The tuned lens learns a translator per layer.\", \"Patchscopes pastes an activation into a new prompt.\", \"LatentQA trains a decoder, which can add its own bias.\", \"Treat readouts as hints, then confirm causally.\"], \"edits\": [], \"strip\": [], \"trap_title\": \"\\\"The lens shows the answer at layer 15, so the model knew it there\\\"\", \"trap_html\": \"<p>It sounds direct, but a lens is a readout you chose, and a learned one can find information the model doesn't use. Decodable at layer 15 isn't the same as used by later layers. Patch or steer at that layer to test it.</p>\"}, \"ai-engineering/probing-classifiers\": {\"tabs\": [\"Intuition\", \"How it works\", \"In practice\"], \"situation\": \"Your probe detects \\\"the model is lying\\\" from layer 30 with 95% accuracy on your test set. The product team wants to ship it as a deception monitor next week. The interviewer asks:\", \"q\": \"A probe detects a property from activations with 95% accuracy. What does that tell you, and what doesn't it?\", \"analogy\": \"A probe is like a metal detector on a beach. A beep tells you there's metal, not that anyone is using it, and a detector that's sensitive enough beeps at bottle caps. Digging is the causal test.\", \"cards\": [\"Decodable, selective, and actually used.\", [[\"Decodable\", [\"High accuracy\", \"from activations\"], \"Not proof of use\", \"info\"], [\"Selectivity\", [\"Real minus\", \"control accuracy\"], \"Memorising?\", \"a\"], [\"Causal\", [\"Erase or patch\", \"the direction\"], \"Is it used?\", \"ok\"]]], \"caption\": \"Three questions to ask of any probe result.\", \"situation_chapters\": [], \"play\": {\"chapter\": \"l3-c2\", \"try\": \"<b>Try it.</b> Make the probe more powerful, and compare its accuracy on real and random labels.\", \"why\": \"<b>Notice:</b> a powerful probe scores well even on random labels, because it memorises. Selectivity, the gap between the two, tells you what the activations really hold.\"}, \"how\": [\"l3-c1\", \"l3-c3\", \"l4-c1\", \"l4-c2\"], \"practice\": [\"l4-c3\", \"l4-c4\", \"new\"], \"trap\": [], \"reference\": [\"src\"], \"checklist\": [\"A probe shows a property is decodable, not that it's used.\", \"Compare against a control task with random labels.\", \"Selectivity = real accuracy − control accuracy.\", \"Prefer simple, linear probes.\", \"Amnesic probing and patching test whether it's used.\", \"Test monitors on new distributions at a fixed false-positive rate.\"], \"edits\": [], \"strip\": [], \"trap_title\": \"\\\"95% accuracy, ship it as a monitor\\\"\", \"trap_html\": \"<p>It sounds strong, but a probe can track a correlate, like whether the text talks about lying, rather than lying itself. Apollo's deception probes scored highly yet partly tracked the topic. Test on realistic, unfamiliar cases at the false-positive rate you can afford, and against a black-box baseline.</p>\"}, \"ai-engineering/steering-vectors\": {\"tabs\": [\"Intuition\", \"How it works\", \"In practice\"], \"situation\": \"Your chatbot is too sycophantic: it agrees with users even when they're wrong. Retraining will take weeks. Someone suggests adding a vector to the activations at inference time. The interviewer asks:\", \"q\": \"How can you change a model's behaviour at inference time without retraining it?\", \"analogy\": \"Think of a mixing desk during a live show. Steering doesn't rewrite the song; it pushes one slider while it plays. Push gently and the mood shifts. Push too far and the whole mix distorts.\", \"cards\": [\"Find it, add it, watch the side effects.\", [[\"Find\", [\"Mean of A\", \"minus mean of B\"], \"Contrast pairs\", \"info\"], [\"Add\", [\"α × vector\", \"at one layer\"], \"No retraining\", \"a\"], [\"Watch\", [\"Too much α\", \"hurts quality\"], \"Tune and test\", \"bad\"]]], \"caption\": \"The same vector can monitor a trait, steer it, or flag training data.\", \"situation_chapters\": [], \"play\": {\"chapter\": \"m3-c2\", \"try\": \"<b>Try it.</b> Add the refusal direction to a harmless request. Then remove it from a harmful one.\", \"why\": \"<b>Notice:</b> one direction switches refusal on and off. That's why steering is both a control knob and a jailbreak risk for open-weight models.\"}, \"how\": [\"m3-c1\", \"m3-c3\"], \"practice\": [\"new\"], \"trap\": [], \"reference\": [\"src\"], \"checklist\": [\"A steering vector is a difference of mean activations.\", \"Add α times it at one layer during inference.\", \"Too much α degrades fluency and capability.\", \"Refusal runs through a single direction in many chat models.\", \"Steering is also a causal test of a feature.\", \"Persona vectors can monitor traits and screen training data.\"], \"edits\": [], \"strip\": [], \"trap_title\": \"\\\"Steering is a free fix, just turn up α\\\"\", \"trap_html\": \"<p>It sounds cheap, but large α pushes activations off distribution and degrades quality, and persona-vector work found inference-time steering can degrade general capabilities. Evaluate the steered model on your normal benchmarks, not only the target behaviour, and consider steering during fine-tuning or fixing the data instead.</p>\"}, \"ai-engineering/knowledge-localisation-and-editing\": {\"tabs\": [\"Intuition\", \"How it works\", \"In practice\"], \"situation\": \"Your model states an outdated fact: a company's CEO who left last year. Retraining is expensive. A teammate suggests using ROME to edit just that fact. The interviewer asks:\", \"q\": \"Where does a fact live in a model, and can you edit it safely?\", \"analogy\": \"Think of correcting one entry in a phone book that's been copied into many other books. Finding the page where the entry matters most doesn't mean it's the best page to fix, and a careless fix can smudge the neighbouring entries.\", \"cards\": [\"Locate, edit, then check what else moved.\", [[\"Trace\", [\"Corrupt, then\", \"restore states\"], \"Where it matters\", \"info\"], [\"Edit\", [\"Rank-one update\", \"to an MLP\"], \"ROME, MEMIT\", \"a\"], [\"Check\", [\"Related and\", \"unrelated facts\"], \"Side effects\", \"bad\"]]], \"caption\": \"Where a fact matters and where to edit it are different questions.\", \"situation_chapters\": [], \"play\": {\"chapter\": \"m2-c2\", \"try\": \"<b>Try it.</b> Corrupt the subject, then restore one hidden state at a time.\", \"why\": \"<b>Notice:</b> restoring middle-layer MLP states at the subject's last token brings the answer back. That inspired ROME, but where a fact matters isn't always the best place to edit it.\"}, \"how\": [\"m2-c1\", \"z1-c2\"], \"practice\": [\"m2-c3\", \"new\"], \"trap\": [], \"reference\": [\"src\"], \"checklist\": [\"Causal tracing corrupts the subject and restores one state at a time.\", \"Facts concentrate in middle-layer MLPs at the subject's last token.\", \"ROME edits one fact with a rank-one update; MEMIT edits many.\", \"Localisation doesn't predict the best layer to edit.\", \"Test edits on paraphrases, related and unrelated facts.\", \"For facts that change, retrieval is usually safer.\"], \"edits\": [], \"strip\": [], \"trap_title\": \"\\\"Causal tracing found the layer, so edit there\\\"\", \"trap_html\": \"<p>It sounds logical, but Hase et al. found tracing gave no insight into which layer is best to edit; the choice of layer mattered far more than the trace. Pick the edit layer by testing edits directly, and measure the damage to related facts.</p>\"}, \"ai-engineering/model-diffing\": {\"tabs\": [\"Intuition\", \"How it works\", \"In practice\"], \"situation\": \"After you fine-tune your model on a narrow task, it starts giving harmful advice in unrelated conversations. Your evaluations catch the symptom, but not why it happened. The interviewer asks:\", \"q\": \"After fine-tuning, how do you find out what changed inside the model?\", \"analogy\": \"Think of comparing two versions of a document with track changes on. The diff shows what was added or strengthened, but if a paragraph was only reworded, a naive diff marks it as brand new.\", \"cards\": [\"Compare base and fine-tuned, carefully.\", [[\"Yardstick\", [\"The base\", \"model's SAE\"], \"What moved\", \"info\"], [\"Crosscoder\", [\"Shared features\", \"for both\"], \"New or shared\", \"a\"], [\"Caution\", [\"Rotation looks\", \"like new\"], \"False positives\", \"bad\"]]], \"caption\": \"Diffing can explain why a fine-tune changed behaviour, and sometimes warn before evals do.\", \"situation_chapters\": [], \"play\": {\"chapter\": \"m1-c2\", \"try\": \"<b>Try it.</b> Rotate the old \\\"sports\\\" feature further during fine-tuning, and watch which features the diff flags as new.\", \"why\": \"<b>Notice:</b> past about 25°, the rotated old feature is flagged as new too, and nothing in the diff output tells them apart. Check new features against the base model's features before reporting them.\"}, \"how\": [\"m1-c1\", \"m1-c3\"], \"practice\": [\"new\"], \"trap\": [], \"reference\": [\"src\"], \"checklist\": [\"Diffing asks what a training step added or amplified.\", \"A base-model SAE works as a fixed yardstick.\", \"Crosscoders map both models into shared features.\", \"Rotated old features can look new.\", \"Without weights, you can only diff outputs.\", \"Diffing can flag risky fine-tunes before evaluations do.\"], \"edits\": [], \"strip\": [], \"trap_title\": \"\\\"New feature in the crosscoder, so fine-tuning taught a new behaviour\\\"\", \"trap_html\": \"<p>It sounds like a finding, but a small rotation of an existing feature can appear as one specific to the new model. Check its similarity with the base model's features, and confirm it by steering before reporting it as new.</p>\"}, \"ai-engineering/interpretability-for-safety\": {\"tabs\": [\"Intuition\", \"How it works\", \"In practice\"], \"situation\": \"Your lab plans to cite a mechanistic audit as evidence that a new model isn't deceptive. A reviewer worries the audit only looked at a small part of the model's computation. The interviewer asks:\", \"q\": \"How is interpretability actually used for AI safety today, and what can go wrong?\", \"analogy\": \"Interpretability for safety is like a medical scan. It can find problems a check-up misses and guide treatment, but a clean scan of 30% of the body doesn't mean the patient is healthy.\", \"cards\": [\"Monitor, intervene, and know the risk.\", [[\"Monitor\", [\"Probes and\", \"feature alerts\"], \"Catch it live\", \"info\"], [\"Intervene\", [\"Steer, erase,\", \"edit\"], \"Fix it inside\", \"a\"], [\"Risk\", [\"False\", \"confidence\"], \"Partial coverage\", \"bad\"]]], \"caption\": \"Useful evidence, never a certificate on its own.\", \"situation_chapters\": [], \"play\": {\"chapter\": \"o2-c2\", \"try\": \"<b>Try it.</b> Score three explanations of the same feature: a precise one, a vague one, and a plausible but wrong one.\", \"why\": \"<b>Notice:</b> the simulator score separates them only because it's tested on new text. Automated explanations need scoring like this, and human spot checks on top.\"}, \"how\": [\"z1-c1\", \"z1-c3\", \"o2-c1\", \"pa-c2\"], \"practice\": [\"z2-c1\", \"z2-c2\", \"z2-c3\", \"z3-c1\", \"new\"], \"trap\": [], \"reference\": [\"src\"], \"checklist\": [\"Black-box safety works but doesn't explain failures.\", \"White-box tools: monitors, steering, erasure, editing.\", \"Self-reports can confabulate or deceive.\", \"Automated interpretability needs scoring and human checks.\", \"A partial audit that finds nothing proves little.\", \"Published mechanisms can also help attackers.\"], \"edits\": [], \"strip\": [], \"trap_title\": \"\\\"The audit found no deception feature, so the model isn't deceptive\\\"\", \"trap_html\": \"<p>It sounds like evidence, but if the methods explain only part of the computation, not finding something proves little. Say what fraction you examined, which methods you used and their known failure modes, and combine it with behavioural and red-team evidence.</p>\"}}")

